---
export const prerender = true;
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import FounderPhoto from '../components/FounderPhoto.astro';
import Certifications from '../components/Certifications.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — TomProtects"
  description="More than twenty years in IT and security, spent doing this work under the same constraints small organizations have. Fractional CISO and security advisor, based in Morgantown, West Virginia."
  footerNewsletter={false}
>
  <Section width="text">
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-6 sm:flex-row sm:items-center sm:gap-8">
      <FounderPhoto class="h-40 w-40 shrink-0" loading="eager" />

      <div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">About</h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink/70">
          I'm Tom. I have spent more than twenty years keeping organizations secure, and I work
          from Morgantown, West Virginia.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        I work with small organizations that have reached the point where security clearly matters
        and no one inside them owns it. In practice that means small professional practices —
        dental, medical, legal, accounting — small nonprofits, and small local government agencies,
        usually somewhere between five and twenty-five people.
      </p>

      <p>
        That situation is more common than it sounds. The organization depends on technology for
        nearly everything, it holds information that matters to the people it belongs to, and
        clients, insurers or a board have started asking questions that are difficult to answer.
        There is usually an IT provider doing capable work, but nobody whose job is to step back and
        decide what should be done about security, and in what order.
      </p>

      <p>
        This practice exists because the usual options do not fit that situation. A full-time
        security leader is more than a small organization needs or can justify. Product vendors
        answer a narrower question than the one being asked. What is actually needed is someone
        experienced to look at the whole picture and help you decide what matters. Some people call
        that a fractional CISO: a part-time security leader, shared across a few organizations
        rather than hired full time. The title matters less than the job.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am based in Morgantown and the practice is deliberately anchored here — most of the work
        is with organizations across north-central West Virginia, close enough that I can sit down
        with you when that is the useful thing to do. I also work remotely, and do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="white" width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Background</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Much of my career has been spent as the sole security and systems lead for a group of
        affiliated operating companies, responsible for everything from the systems people used
        every day to the security decisions nobody else was going to make. I have handled real
        incidents, not only planned for them.
      </p>

      <p>
        That shapes how I advise. It is straightforward to recommend good security when someone
        else has to find the time and money for it. Having made those calls myself, with a limited
        budget, a small team, and a business that had to keep running while security improved,
        keeps my advice grounded in what can actually be done.
      </p>

      <h3 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Certifications</h3>

      <p>
        CISSP, CISM, CDPSE, and PMP. They are worth stating plainly because they show the work has
        been measured against a standard. Judgment matters more than any of them, and no
        certification demonstrates that on its own.
      </p>

      <Certifications size={52} label="Certification badges, verified on Credly" class="pt-1" />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section width="text">
    <h2 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">What makes this different</h2>

    <div class="mt-6 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-ink/70">
      <p>
        Most security advice fails for one of two reasons. It is either too generic to act on, or
        it is written for an organization ten times the size of yours. Useful advice depends on
        knowing which parts of a framework apply to an organization your size and which parts can
        wait until you are much larger.
      </p>

      <p>
        So the work is practical. Recommendations come with an order, a reason, and a realistic
        sense of effort. I explain them in language your leadership or your board can follow,
        because a recommendation nobody understands is a recommendation nobody funds. And because I
        work on both sides of this, the operational side and the technical side, I can talk to your
        IT provider and to the people who sign off on the spending without either conversation
        getting lost.
      </p>

      <p>
        I am not here to sell you software, and I am not going to scare you into a retainer. The
        goal is an organization that knows where it stands, has a plan it believes in, and can
        explain both to anyone who asks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section background="ink" width="text">
    <NewsletterSignup id="about" variant="panel" onDark buttonLabel="Send me the checklist" />
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>
